A1. This is always digging in. ~ MOLE
A4. These are big occasions. ~ FETES
A9. This works against the Union. ~ SCAB
A13. This is all wool. ~ EWE
A14. Night clothes (Plural) ~ TUXEDOS
A15. To employ. ~ USE
A16. This is over the head of a dead one. ~ SLAB
A18. Adjective, feminine, and decidedly singular. ~ STAID
A19. Speed demons often fail to pass this. ~ TEST
A20. A fellow who is bound to rise. ~ FLIER
A21. Many men like them hot. ~ CAKES
A23. What sea-dogs are often called. ~ SALTS
A26. The saxophone player in the next apartment has a lot of this ~ BRASS
A29. What some have been known to break out with. ~ FILES
A32. It is a bad policy—to let this happen. ~ LAPSE
A33. What any college boy would do to get his allowance raised. ~ ASK
A35. The perfect thing—never attained. ~ IDEAL
A36. A friend of Satan. ~ IMP
A37. The bridge that hath no ending. ~ AUCTION
A38. Part of the verb to be. ~ ARE
A39. This is apt to cause an exposure ~ SPLIT
A41. This makes a lot of noise in the kitchen. ~ TIN
A42. A Yankee soldier did this—and was pardoned by Abraham Lincoln. ~ DOSED
A44. These have to be put down before they come up. ~ SEEDS
A45. These are always taking action. ~ SUERS
A46. Anyone who holds a contrary religious belief. ~ PAGAN
A51. Etiquette demands this be forked over ~ SALAD
A52. What the fan calls the erring ball-player ~ NAMES
A55. These do their sleep-walking vicariously. ~ TOTS
A57. Society hounds travel in these ~ PACKS
A59. A noun, usually implying the adjective high. ~ RATE
A61. What the superstitious are inspired with. ~ AWE
A62. The hardest kind of a job for a lazy man. ~ REGULAR
A63. The field of the poets. ~ LEA
A64. These are raised yearly by fashion designers. ~ HEMS
A65. Any member of the minority. ~ REBEL
A66. One way to see through something. ~ PEEP

D1. What soldiers get for dinner. ~ MESS
D2. This bird stays up all night. ~ OWL
D3. This lets go in the fall. ~ LEAF
D4. These are good for blow-outs. ~ FUSES
D5. This is always falling for a movie star. ~ EXTRA
D6. A famous party of pre-revolution days. ~ TEA
D7. A proclamation. ~ EDICT
D8. Suckers often buy these. ~ SODAS
D10. Ball directors. ~ CUES
D11. This is usually heavy laden. ~ ASS
D12. These are always a gamble. ~ BETS
D17. What the Shepherd likes to do for his flock. ~ BLESS
D19. This kind of a stew isn't so hot. ~ TEPID
D22. What the new papa is at three o'clock in the morning. ~ TRAMPER
D24. What our salary is not in the habit of doing. ~ LASTING
D25. What men have been since the day of Adam. ~ WEARERS
D26. This should go with a kiss. ~ BLISS
D27. This struck Newton with an idea. ~ APPLE
D28. The commuter's stand by. ~ SEATS
D29. What a gold mine and an oil well would be—if discovered on your property. ~ FINDS
D30. A disagreement between landlord and tenant. ~ LEASE
D31. Ice boats. ~ SLEDS
D33. This comes between curtains. ~ ACT
D34. Mom, pop, aunty—and the rest of 'em. ~ KIN
D40. It takes head work to get these. ~ IDEAS
D43. What edge of a thing is on the border. ~ OUTER
D46. A dirt conveyor. ~ PAPER
D47. A proverb. ~ ADAGE
D48. The height of sex-appeal in 1900. ~ ANKLE
D49. Nosy. ~ NASAL
D50. Where marital troubles are multiplied. ~ UTAH
D51. This is pushing up the daisies (Singular). ~ STEM
D53. This causes the wife to go buy-buy. ~ SALE
D54. This causes one to leave the earth for a short time. ~ LEAP
D56. You've got to give a person credit who wants to do this to you. ~ OWE
D58. A black babe in the woods. ~ CUB
D60. Where the dub's golf ball rests in peace. ~ TEE


